comment.narrative:
This has to be interpreted as follows: 
1) a baseline for this indicator was not available;
2) the task team decided during mid-term review to use as baseline the mid-term review survey data (the only data available), namely 44% (44% of women beneficiaries reported that they had a say in community decision-making);
3) the recent IBM3 survey gave as a result 51.2% (of women beneficiaries reported that they have a say in community decision-making), thus meaning that the data to be reported in this ISR for this indicator is 7.2%, being the increase in women participation in decision making (with end target being 20% increase compared to baseline).
